WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.860
This episode is sponsored by Blinkist.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.920
Blinkist puts all the need to know information
from thousands of nonfiction books and condenses

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:16.187
them down into just 15 minutes, and you can
go to blinkist.com/microcosmos to learn more.

00:00:23.675 --> 00:00:29.390
Hi, it’s me, Deboki, back on the Cnidarian
beat.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.850
Last time I was here, we explored the terrifying
world of a hydra’s tentacles.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:40.650
Today, I’m here to talk about one of their
relatives, the starlet sea anemone, also known

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.780
as Nematostella vectensis.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:51.809
I didn’t know that I’ve spent my entire
life mispronouncing “anemone” as “anenome”

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:55.539
until I began learning about the starlet sea
anemone.

00:00:55.539 --> 00:01:01.329
And so, I will often be referring to them
as Nematostella throughout this episode.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:04.019
I hope you understand.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.460
I can’t fault the starlet sea anemone for
its name though.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.069
After all, I have a name that often involves
correcting other’s pronunciation.

00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:19.779
And there’s more to the Nematostella’s
life that I feel kinship with, but to get

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:23.409
there, I need to start with a question.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:28.670
When you look at the starlet sea anemone,
where do you think it’s from?

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.060
The water, sure.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:38.119
But water is vast, vaster than land, and it
has its own boundaries and geography.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:44.289
So, like, where do you think it’s really
from?

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:48.229
We can tell you where this particular individual
is from.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:54.659
A lab at the University of Vienna, where it
lived until a scientist named Sanjay sent

00:01:54.659 --> 00:02:02.409
one of his samples to James—our master of
microscopes—so that he, and we, could look at them.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:08.690
So thank you, Sanjay.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.040
There are a lot of nematostellas in labs around
the world.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.760
Their bodies and reproduction are simple,
providing us with a means to explore the questions

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:25.299
and secrets behind how patterns and layers
form in bodies across nature.

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:32.170
But before the nematostella ended up in labs,
they—or one of their forebears—must have

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.859
come from somewhere.

00:02:33.859 --> 00:02:36.500
But where?

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.159
The answer should be simple.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:46.010
In 1935, the biologist Thomas Alan Stephenson
gathered twenty years worth of work into a

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.810
two-volume monograph titled “The British
Sea Anemones”.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:57.470
In there lies the first recorded description
of the starlet sea anemone, which had been

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:03.530
found in a brackish pond on the Isle of Wight
.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:09.109
Compared to the organisms we usually focus
on, the nematostella is quite large, growing

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:11.720
several millimeters in height.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.290
But for someone wading through a pond, it’d
be easy to overlook.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:23.170
It’s small, translucent, and a burrower,
digging itself into mud and letting its tentacles

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.139
spill out onto the visible surface.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:33.989
But difficult as the nematostella is to spot,
researchers became concerned in the 1980s

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:38.609
when their counts of the starlet sea anemone
in England began to dwindle.

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:47.060
In 1983, the nematostella was classified as
a vulnerable species to protect it from extinction.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:53.599
This is sadly not an uncommon tale when it
comes to our interactions with wildlife, and

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:58.829
so it’s tempting to leave it here,
to allow the starlet sea anemone to become

00:03:58.829 --> 00:04:04.670
yet another cautionary tale of our environmental
misdeeds.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.749
And yet.

00:04:07.749 --> 00:04:13.090
While the species was first described in Britain,
it has been found in other parts of the world

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:19.910
too, particularly on both the Atlantic and
Pacific coasts of North America.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:27.100
And when scientists counted, there seemed
to be plenty of nematostella in these other regions.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:31.479
But there were some weird quirks to these
different populations.

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:39.070
The English Nematostella population seemed
to be completely void of males, like an underwater Themiscyra.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.470
Meanwhile, the population in Nova Scotia seemed
to be entirely male.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:50.710
And there didn’t seem to be any environmental
explanation for why some populations might

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:53.050
be different from the others.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:59.460
So what caused these nematostella to take
up at such different locations, and in such

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.159
different ways?

00:05:02.159 --> 00:05:03.533
Boats.

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:14.759
For centuries , boats have been traveling
the world, taking cargo and passengers alike.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:20.849
And with them have travelled even smaller
passengers, including—most likely—the nematostella.

00:05:20.849 --> 00:05:27.270
After all, it’s small and hardy, able to
survive journeys through the different temperatures

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.070
and salinities.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:35.919
And most importantly for the traveling nematostella,
it can reproduce asexually, dividing to form

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:38.610
more versions of itself.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:44.169
This would explain why we might find populations
of the starlet sea anemone that are entirely

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:46.690
female or entirely male.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:53.060
That’s just the one that showed up first,
the one that made its way to a new home and

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.669
then filled it with its own clones.

00:05:56.669 --> 00:06:02.740
Our genes are a map of our ancestor’s lives,
and in the case of the nematostella, they’ve

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.720
helped scientists chart the path the organism
likely took.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.000
The nematostella in Britain, for example,
have very little genetic diversity compared

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.210
to their New England counterparts, suggesting
that the British ones are a more recent transplant.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:24.230
And using inferences like that, scientists
have piece together a story where nematostella

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:30.289
made its way from the western Atlantic (or,
maybe less confusingly, the east coast of

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:35.200
North America), and then travelled the world
from there.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.569
So the nematostella is not native to Britain.

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:43.580
It’s a recent arrival, early in forming
its identity.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:49.480
But when it comes to asking where the starlet
sea anemone is from, I’ll be straightforward:

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.720
it’s hard not to project a little as someone
who has been asked that question in its many

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:01.430
forms before, and as someone whose home is
an amalgamation of the immediate places I’ve

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:06.860
lived in, and a city on the other side of
the world where I didn’t.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:13.129
Even learning about how the genetic uniformity
of certain populations provides us snapshots

00:07:13.129 --> 00:07:16.560
of the anemone’s journey feels familiar.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.629
Immigration and diasporas are complex and
nuanced, so there’s no way to generalize

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:25.070
from my own experience.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:30.220
But an idea that I heard once and that has
stuck with me when I understand my Indian

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:37.159
American experience is this notion that first
and second generations like me tend to carry

00:07:37.159 --> 00:07:43.669
our family’s country of origin with us from
a certain point of time, so that the traditions

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:51.400
and rituals we choose to maintain in this
new home tie us together while also inevitably dating us.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:57.689
Often, when you’re a first or second generation
immigrant in America, your journey is framed

00:07:57.689 --> 00:08:03.240
in terms of tension: of a tension between
who claims you, and who you claim.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:09.449
I hate thinking in these terms, even when
it feels true because I’ve come to realize

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:15.960
that as much as these claims are impactful,
they can also be arbitrary, shifting with

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.460
the currents and climate and location.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:27.120
If it’s a question of where I belong, the
answer will always be conditional, and that’s frustrating.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:34.890
Defining the Nematostella’s origins is conditional
as well, dependent upon the asker’s aims.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:42.040
But the Nematostella is not an immigrant,
and I have no desire to compare the entirety

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:47.760
of the immigrant experience and its human
realities to an anemone.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:55.170
As a question of conservation though, the
nematostella’s origins become significant.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:09:02.570
In England, where our understanding of the
species originated, the populations are dwindling.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:08.291
But the efforts to protect it are built more
on earlier assumptions that the nematostella

00:09:08.291 --> 00:09:13.640
is a native species, which we know now it
is not.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:20.730
But we also know that whenever the nematostella
arrived at its new home, it took to it, and

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:25.030
without causing further harm to its local
ecology.

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:31.060
In the early 2000s, scientists argued that
while we may not need to introduce populations

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:36.410
of nematostella to its British locations,
it is still worth protecting the ones that

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.820
are already there.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.090
So where is the nematostella from?

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.870
Well, it depends.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:51.780
And this time, I find that kind of beautiful.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:57.530
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

